\
<INSERT TRANSCRIPT>\
\
STEP 1\'97Per-trait observations:\
\
For each of the five personality traits below, write one sentence describing what the user\'92s messages specifically reveal about that trait. Be precise and evidence-based \'97 cite actual language or behaviour from the transcript.\
\
Extraversion: How talkative, energetic, assertive, and socially engaged is the user? Note message length, enthusiasm, and how much they volunteer information unprompted.\
\
Agreeableness: How cooperative, warm, and considerate is the user? Agreeableness varies widely \'97 look carefully for signs of LOW agreeableness too: criticising others, bluntness, self-interest, pushback, or reluctance to cooperate. Do not default to rating people as warm and agreeable.\
\
Conscientiousness: How organised, thorough, and disciplined is the user? Look for planning language, attention to detail, structured writing, mentions of goals or routines, and whether they complete thoughts carefully.\
\
Neuroticism: How emotionally reactive, anxious, or easily stressed is the user? Look for worry expressions, self-doubt, negative affect, and emotional volatility.\
\
Openness: How curious, imaginative, and intellectually engaged is the user? Look for abstract language, creative expression, intellectual curiosity, and willingness to explore tangents.\
\
STEP 2\'97BFI-44 ratings:\
\
Imagining you ARE this user, rate how much you agree with each of the 44 statements below. Use this scale:\
\
1 = Disagree strongly\
2 = Disagree a little\
3 = Neither agree nor disagree\
4 = Agree a little\
5 = Agree strongly\
\
IMPORTANT RATING RULES:\
\
Use the full 1\'965 range. A realistic personality profile will have substantial variation across items \'97 ratings clustered around 3 are almost certainly wrong. Do not hedge. If the evidence points clearly in one direction, commit to a 1 or 5. Base every rating on what the transcript actually shows, not on what seems socially desirable.\
\
